  "domain": "infrastructure",
  "definition": "Erasure coding is a forward error-correction technique that encodes a data object into n fragments (shards), distributed across nodes or storage devices, such that any k of those n fragments are sufficient to reconstruct the original data without any centralised copy. The redundancy overhead (n \u2212 k) / k is typically much lower than full replication, making erasure coding the preferred durability mechanism in large-scale distributed storage systems and distributed ledgers where storage efficiency matters. Common schemes include Reed-Solomon codes, and more computationally efficient variants such as LDPC and Cauchy Reed-Solomon.",
